{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedHeight","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedHeight","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Rename","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.CommRing","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Abs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Int"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_le_of_mul_separated_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_le_of_mul_separated_right","kind":"theorem","line":176,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedHeight.lean#L176-L197","doc":"**`H(V) ≤ H(U · V)`** for a non-zero integer `U` in disjoint variables — the mirror of\n`abs_coeff_le_of_mul_separated`.\n\nAn earlier version of this module deferred this one, on the grounds that nothing called for\nit.  The induction step of Roth's lemma calls for it: it bounds *both* factors of the\nseparated Wronskian, not just the first. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_le_of_mul_separated","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_le_of_mul_separated","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedHeight.lean#L147-L174","doc":"**`H(U) ≤ H(U · V)`** for a non-zero integer `V` in disjoint variables — the direction the\ninduction step of Roth's lemma needs, and the one bean `qou-4lbh` predicted would require\nGelfond's inequality.\n\nIt requires nothing of the kind.  Choose any `j` in the support of `V`; then `coeff j V` is a\nnon-zero integer, so `1 ≤ |coeff j V|`, and\n\n    |coeff i U|  ≤  |coeff i U| · |coeff j V|  =  |coeff (i ⊞ j) (U · V)|  ≤  B.\n\nThe middle step is the identity, not an estimate.  Gelfond bounds the heights of factors of a\nproduct in *shared* variables, where the coefficients genuinely mix; in disjoint variables\nthey do not mix, and the bound is free. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_mul_separated_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_mul_separated_le","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedHeight.lean#L137-L145","doc":"**`H(U · V) ≤ H(U) · H(V)`**, one coefficient at a time.\n\nImmediate from the identity: every coefficient of the product is a single product `uᵢvⱼ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.coeff_sumElim_rename_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.coeff_sumElim_rename_mul","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedHeight.lean#L88-L135","doc":"**The identity this module rests on.**  For disjoint variable groups the coefficients of a\nproduct are *exactly* the products of the coefficients:\n\n    coeff (i ⊞ j) (U · V) = coeff i U * coeff j V\n\n`coeff_mul` sums over the antidiagonal of `i ⊞ j`, but a summand can be non-zero only if its\nfirst component is supported on `σ` and its second on `τ` — and there is exactly one such\nsplitting.  Every other term dies, so no estimate is involved and the result is an equality.\n\nStated over any `CommRing`; no domain hypothesis, because the vanishing of the other terms is\nestablished factor-by-factor rather than through `mul_ne_zero_iff`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.eq_sumElim","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.eq_sumElim","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedHeight.lean#L78-L86","doc":"Every exponent vector on `σ ⊕ τ` splits, so the identity below covers *all* coefficients\nof the product, not just those of a special shape. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sumElim_eq_mapDomain_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sumElim_eq_mapDomain_add","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedHeight.lean#L61-L76","doc":"The exponent vector `Finsupp.sumElim i j` on `σ ⊕ τ` is the sum of the two pushed-forward\nvectors — the bookkeeping that lets `coeff_mul`'s antidiagonal be evaluated. ","signatureChunk":0}]}